[Music]  the milkis rs.1000 isn't a car for  everyday driving its suspension is very  hard and there's little noise damping  still this vehicle is something special  it was the only series production sports  car ever made in communist East Germany  volkmar crew spec owns the last one ever  made he says what makes the car fun to  drive is that it's just over 1 metre  tall so you're literally sitting 10  centimeters over the road and have a  very interesting perspective and of  course there's the mid-engine with his  rear transmission he says weather and  other conditions permitting the car  drifts nicely 101 Mel cous RS were built  between 1969 in 1984 many Vaught bug  parts went into their construction  that's why the milkis is often called a  wolf and sheep's clothing because it  looks faster than it actually is  depending on the engine it can reach  between 160 and 210 kilometers an hour  [Music]  Meyer explains at height smokeless  developed the car in 1969 it was East  Germany's answer to Western racecars the  RS 1000 had a fiberglass body with an  aluminum skin had used lots of standard  parts from the varberg 353 like this  turn signal the cars chassis was based  on that model - Chris Peak says here  there's the steel frame with independent  suspension there are the disc brakes on  the front axle it's all mechanically  operated with no brake booster no power  assisted steering  the cars front brakes wheel suspension  floor pan and braking system are also  taken from the Varberg 353 the Goldwing  doors give the milkis an exotic look for  a communist-era vehicle Chris big points  out the two separate gas tanks above the  rocker panel one on the left and one on  the right which holds 25 liters apiece  the steering instruments transmission  and electronics are also borrowed from  Bart board who speak shows us another  special thing about the malkos the ride  height of the seats can be adjusted  after lifting the seat to the desired  position  a wooden board is laid under it here  it's high now it's well it's practical  and simply crisp big notes that the  engine is located ahead of the rear axle  and the transmission behind this model  from 1984 was fitted with a 102  horsepower engine with a kerb weight of  680 kilos that made for a sporty Drive  he's in fox-like but this car's engine  isn't the original one instead of the  standard Varberg 3 cylinder this mochas  is powered by a four-cylinder BMW engine  plenty of power combined with a  lightweight makes for great driving  performance but drivers should be  careful not to push the Milkha's past  its limits  [Music]  Chris peak says the cars acceleration is  a real treat but he warns you need to  keep your hands firmly on the steering  wheel and always be ready to brake  because as he said before the car has no  brake boosters and the engine is a bit  too powerful for the series production  Varberg brakes anglish 30 burns in the  autumn is arrogant but for some Vinick  people both overseas also house max from  chi design because we take the party's  in fatso  regular maintenance is also very  important  Chris big always gives his milkis a  thorough checkup before a long trip if  the car has a powerful engine and runs  in the high revs so it's wise to check  the oil before setting out it's a relief  if the cars not using too much oil that  crews big nose all as well the last one  is ok I'm done quitting life to be on  the safe side crispy adds a bit of  coolant and wipes the radiator cap  then he checks the oil and finally the  trunk lid which can spring open while  the car is in motion  krispy says that can happen if you don't  monitor the tension of the Bowden cables  then the trunk can fly open it goes down  again but it's an unpleasant surprise he  ensures the cables are pre-loaded to  avoid this he also checks the insulation  here which sometimes comes loose  especially in hot weather but it's in  place so everything's wonderful  due to its stiff suspension the milk is  oversteers when taking curves at high  speed so drivers need to be very  reactive Chris pig likes the way the car  holds the road it's wonderful in dry  weather but when it's wet you need to be  careful as it tends to oversteer  otherwise the handling is very good  personally he thinks 150 kilometers an  hour is fast enough though you can go  180 or 190 but then he says you feel  uplift at the front and would be well  advised to take your foot off the gas he  thinks 130 to 150 kilometers an hour is  plenty fast for a car that is  essentially of art bore  there are some 80 mill coos rs.1000 s  around so grab one while you can those  in good condition sell for around 60,000  euros in Germany

